  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)-{tan}^{2}\u2061x & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.\n$$",
